WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.130
On the morning of the first of November, seventeen fifty-five, every church in Lisbon was ablaze with candles, and half the city was at prayer.

2
00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:12.260
At twenty minutes to ten, the ground shook three times.

3
00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:20.870
When the survivors ran to the riverbank to escape, they found that the waters of the Tagus had drawn back, laying bare the shipwrecks on the riverbed.

4
00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:23.240
Then the sea came back.

5
00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.690
In the autumn of seventeen fifty-five, Lisbon was one of the richest ports in Europe.

6
00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:44.660
The city spread across the slopes of seven hills on the northern shore of the broad estuary where the Tagus River opens into the Atlantic Ocean.

7
00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:47.990
Its population stood at around two hundred thousand.

8
00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:55.800
That figure placed it among the most populous cities on the continent, alongside a handful of others such as London, Paris and Naples.

9
00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:06.010
Ships docking in its harbor brought gold and diamonds from Brazil; spices, silk and porcelain from India and the Far East; and ivory from the coasts of Africa.

10
00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:13.020
From the same harbor sailed the ships that carried enslaved people, taken by force to the far side of the Atlantic.

11
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.830
Lisbon's wealth had this dark face as well.

12
00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.680
The real fortune came from Brazil.

13
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:27.170
Veins of gold discovered in the Minas Gerais region at the start of the century had brought the Portuguese crown an extraordinary income.

14
00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:32.100
The previous king, John the Fifth, had spent this money lavishly.

15
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:39.750
He built an enormous palace and monastery at Mafra, north of Lisbon, and brought works of art, books and holy relics from Rome.

16
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:45.000
The great aqueduct that carried clean water into the city was also a work of his reign.

17
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:51.370
Its pointed arches, spanning the Alcântara valley, were considered the pride of the engineering of the age.

18
00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:56.140
In seventeen fifty, his son Joseph the First took the throne.

19
00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.830
The new king loved music and opera.

20
00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:05.360
Beside the Ribeira Palace, the Riverside Palace, on the banks of the river, he had an opera house built.

21
00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:10.610
Known as the Tagus Opera, the building opened in the spring of seventeen fifty-five.

22
00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:17.140
It was spoken of as one of the most magnificent theaters of its time, but it would stand for only a few months.

23
00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:20.950
The Ribeira Palace was the heart of the city.

24
00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:24.760
The broad square in front of it was called the Palace Square.

25
00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:30.970
One side of the square looked directly onto the river, and ships would moor almost at the foot of the palace.

26
00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:35.420
The palace library was estimated to hold seventy thousand volumes.

27
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:39.550
On its walls hung paintings by Titian, Rubens and Correggio.

28
00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:48.800
Its archives preserved documents from Portugal's Age of Discovery: detailed records of Vasco da Gama's voyages, maps and ships' logs.

29
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.490
Nearby stood the India House.

30
00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:58.660
In the warehouses of this institution, which managed the eastern trade, lay piles of spices and cloth.

31
00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:03.910
Behind the palace and the square, narrow, winding streets climbed toward the hills.

32
00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:09.480
This medieval street pattern was interwoven with neighborhoods dating back to the Moorish era.

33
00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:14.890
The houses leaned against one another, some rising five or six stories high.

34
00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:20.620
Above the streets rose the towers of hundreds of churches, monasteries and chapels.

35
00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:23.470
Lisbon was a deeply devout city.

36
00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:29.200
The Inquisition was still at work, and religious ceremonies set the rhythm of the city's calendar.

37
00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.650
At the edge of Rossio Square stood the great Royal Hospital of All Saints.

38
00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:38.580
Up on the hill, the Gothic church of the Carmo Convent looked down over the city.

39
00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:41.430
Not only the Portuguese lived in the city.

40
00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:47.640
English merchants, drawn by port wine and the wool trade, had formed a large colony.

41
00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:53.530
French, Italian, Dutch and German merchants, clergy and sailors wandered the harbor.

42
00:03:53.530 --> 00:04:01.820
Known as much for its religious traditions as for its trade in gold and spices, this capital considered itself one of the safest places in the world.

43
00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:04.990
That year, the first of November fell on a Saturday.

44
00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:11.520
In the Catholic calendar, the first of November is All Saints' Day, the great feast on which all the saints are remembered.

45
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:17.570
Early in the morning, candles were lit in every church in the city, and altars were decked with flowers.

46
00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:22.340
Most of the population had put on their finest clothes to attend morning Mass.

47
00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:25.030
The weather was clear and calm.

48
00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:29.640
The royal family, however, was not in the city that morning.

49
00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:39.690
They had attended Mass at an early hour, and then, at the princesses' request, set off to spend the holiday at their summer residences on the riverbank in Belém, to the west of the city.

50
00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:43.980
It was an ordinary family decision, but it would save the king's life.

51
00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:47.950
At twenty minutes to ten, the ground began to shake.

52
00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:51.120
The epicenter of the earthquake lay far from the city.

53
00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:59.890
According to what we know today, it was in the Atlantic Ocean, some two hundred kilometers off Cape Saint Vincent, at the southwestern tip of Portugal.

54
00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:06.900
In this region, where the African and Eurasian plates meet, accumulated stress had been released all at once.

55
00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:11.990
There were no seismographs at the time, so the magnitude could only be estimated later.

56
00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:17.080
Scientists generally put the magnitude of the earthquake between eight point five and nine.

57
00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:22.650
In other words, this was one of the largest earthquakes ever recorded in European history.

58
00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:26.780
Witnesses described the shaking as arriving in several waves.

59
00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:28.830
First came a faint tremor.

60
00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:32.320
Some thought a heavy cart was rumbling down the street.

61
00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:34.530
Then the main blow struck.

62
00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:40.740
According to the records, the shaking lasted somewhere between three and a half and six minutes in total.

63
00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:44.870
For an earthquake, that is an extraordinarily long time.

64
00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:51.560
Houses first swayed back and forth, then their walls began to split apart and their roofs to cave in.

65
00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:57.290
In the city center, cracks opened in the ground, some of them as much as five meters wide.

66
00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:03.820
The cloud of dust that rose was so thick that witnesses said a sunny morning turned to twilight within minutes.

67
00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:06.990
The greatest loss of life came in the churches.

68
00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:12.880
Stone vaults, domes and bell towers collapsed onto the crowds gathered for the holy day mass.

69
00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:17.650
Most of the city's great churches were either completely destroyed or badly damaged.

70
00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:21.940
The towers of Lisbon Cathedral came down.

71
00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:27.830
On the hill above, the roof of the church of the Carmo Convent fell in on the people inside.

72
00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:33.080
The church's pointed arches still stand today, like a skeleton open to the sky.

73
00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:38.650
Those who tried to flee were buried in the narrow streets as building fronts fell on top of them.

74
00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:42.460
The streets were so narrow that the rubble blocked them in an instant.

75
00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:47.870
The Ribeira Palace was destroyed, along with its precious library and archives.

76
00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:53.600
The Tagus Opera House, opened only a few months earlier, was razed to the ground.

77
00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:56.610
The warehouses of the India House collapsed.

78
00:06:56.610 --> 00:07:03.140
Some accounts say the newly built marble quay sank into the water along with the people who had taken refuge on it.

79
00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:07.270
The details of this story, however, vary from source to source.

80
00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:13.320
Most of the survivors had two thoughts at once: get away from the falling buildings and reach open ground.

81
00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:18.890
The widest open space in the city was the riverfront, Palace Square and the quays.

82
00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:26.700
Thousands ran toward the water, dragging their wounded, carrying their children in their arms, clutching whatever belongings they could grab.

83
00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:29.230
It looked safe.

84
00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:34.480
There were no walls to fall on them, only the river and the ships ahead.

85
00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:42.130
Letters written afterward by English merchants, clergymen and diplomats who were in the city carry those minutes down to us today.

86
00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:50.740
Many of these letters describe a similar scene: crowds kneeling in prayer, begging for their sins to be forgiven, gathering around the priests.

87
00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:54.070
Many people believed the end of the world had come.

88
00:07:54.070 --> 00:08:01.560
A disaster that struck on a holy day morning, inside the churches, in the middle of mass, seemed like a sign in itself.

89
00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:07.130
But the crowd gathered on the shore did not know that a far greater danger was about to arrive.

90
00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:12.860
Some time after the earthquake, the people on the riverbank noticed something strange.

91
00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:15.550
The water was pulling back.

92
00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:20.480
The mouth of the Tagus River was draining fast, and the shoreline was slipping away.

93
00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:29.890
A sight no one had ever seen before was revealed: the riverbed itself, old shipwrecks sunk in the mud, cargo that had fallen into the water years before.

94
00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:33.540
Some people walked out onto the mud out of curiosity.

95
00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:35.750
Then the sea came back.

96
00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:44.680
About forty minutes after the earthquake, a giant wave from the ocean, a tsunami, entered the mouth of the Tagus and pushed upriver against the current toward the city.

97
00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:48.810
The waves came one after another, several times.

98
00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:53.580
In Lisbon, the water is estimated to have risen by about six meters.

99
00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:57.870
It swallowed the quays, the square and the streets near the shore.

100
00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:03.600
A large part of the crowd that had taken refuge on the waterfront lost their lives in these waves.

101
00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:09.170
Ships in the harbor smashed into one another; some capsized, others were driven aground.

102
00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:14.100
As the water drew back, it carried people, belongings and debris away with it.

103
00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:17.750
The tsunami did not strike Lisbon alone.

104
00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:23.640
In the Algarve, on Portugal's southern coast, the waves are known to have climbed much higher.

105
00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:27.130
Coastal towns such as Lagos suffered heavy damage.

106
00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:30.940
In Spain, the coast of Cádiz was battered by the waves.

107
00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:37.310
On the far side of the Strait of Gibraltar, people also died in cities along Morocco's Atlantic coast.

108
00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:45.440
Hours later, the waves reached the coast of Cornwall in southwestern England, Ireland, and even the Caribbean on the other side of the ocean.

109
00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:53.410
In lakes in Sweden and Norway, and at Loch Lomond in Scotland, the water was recorded sloshing about for no apparent reason.

110
00:09:53.410 --> 00:10:01.700
Even in the far corners of Europe, people sensed that morning that something had happened, but they would only learn what it was from letters that arrived weeks later.

111
00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:06.950
In Lisbon, meanwhile, the third face of the disaster had already begun: fire.

112
00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:12.680
That morning, thousands of candles had been lit for the holy day in every church in the city.

113
00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:15.690
Hearths were burning in the homes.

114
00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:23.180
Candles and cooking fires buried under the collapsed buildings spread to toppled wooden beams, to curtains, to fabrics.

115
00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:26.510
Small fires broke out all across the city.

116
00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:30.160
Before long, they merged into a great firestorm.

117
00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:34.610
With the narrow streets choked with rubble, putting it out was impossible.

118
00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:38.420
The wind carried the flames from one neighborhood to the next.

119
00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:42.550
The fire burned for days, for six days by some accounts.

120
00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:47.160
Many of the buildings that had survived the earthquake were now lost to the flames.

121
00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:52.250
The books, paintings and maps left in the ruins of the Ribeira Palace burned.

122
00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:57.660
A large share of the documents from Portugal's Age of Discovery was lost forever.

123
00:10:57.660 --> 00:11:03.230
The Royal Hospital of All Saints in Rossio Square also went up in flames.

124
00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:07.200
Hundreds of patients who were unable to flee died in their beds.

125
00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:14.050
In the homes of wealthy merchants and nobles, gold, silver and jewels melted and mixed into the rubble.

126
00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:18.180
Amid the chaos, another danger appeared.

127
00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:22.310
The city's prisons had collapsed, and the prisoners had escaped.

128
00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:27.080
Reports spread of burning houses and abandoned shops being looted.

129
00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:31.850
The survivors fled to the fields, the hills and the open ground outside the city.

130
00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:38.540
In the November cold, under the open sky, they waited, often with nothing but the clothes on their backs.

131
00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:43.950
Aftershocks continued for weeks, and each one set off a fresh wave of panic.

132
00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:46.800
We do not know the exact death toll.

133
00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:49.650
Most of the records had burned.

134
00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:54.740
Some of the dead had been swept out to sea, and others lay buried beneath the ruins.

135
00:11:54.740 --> 00:11:59.510
Historians' estimates for Lisbon range from thirty thousand to fifty thousand.

136
00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:06.680
In a city of roughly two hundred thousand people, that means losing somewhere between a quarter and a sixth of the population.

137
00:12:06.680 --> 00:12:12.890
The dead in the rest of Portugal, in Spain and in Morocco must be added to that figure.

138
00:12:12.890 --> 00:12:17.820
Most of the city's buildings had been destroyed, burned or left unusable.

139
00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:21.150
The royal family was safe in Belém.

140
00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:25.440
But the shock that José the First lived through was not only physical.

141
00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:30.850
After the disaster, the king became afraid of living within stone walls.

142
00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:35.460
For a long time, the court lived in tents and wooden pavilions.

143
00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:39.270
Eventually a wooden palace was built on the hill of Ajuda.

144
00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:45.000
Known among the people as the Royal Shack, it served as the king's palace for the rest of his life.

145
00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:49.930
While the city burned and the king waited in his tent, someone had to take charge.

146
00:12:49.930 --> 00:12:53.900
That man was Sebastião José de Carvalho e Melo.

147
00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:59.630
History knows him better by the title he received years later: the Marquis of Pombal.

148
00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:05.360
In seventeen fifty-five he was fifty-six years old and served as the king's foreign minister.

149
00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:09.010
He had earlier been a diplomat in London and in Vienna.

150
00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:14.100
He had seen Europe's styles of government, its trade and its new ideas up close.

151
00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:17.590
He was a harsh, determined and fearless administrator.

152
00:13:17.590 --> 00:13:23.800
According to a story often told, the king, in despair after the disaster, asked what should be done.

153
00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:28.090
The answer was short and clear: bury the dead and feed the living.

154
00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:32.540
For centuries these words have been attributed to Pombal.

155
00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:39.390
Some historians, however, believe they were actually spoken by another member of the court, the Marquis of Alorna.

156
00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:45.440
Whoever said it, the sentence summed up the program of the weeks that followed, and it was Pombal who carried it out.

157
00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:48.130
The first danger was disease.

158
00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:51.780
Thousands of bodies lay beneath the ruins and in the streets.

159
00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:57.510
Burying them all in the traditional way, in church graveyards, was impossible.

160
00:13:57.510 --> 00:14:00.680
It would have taken too long, and there was no room.

161
00:14:00.680 --> 00:14:05.450
With the Church's approval, Pombal turned to an unusual solution.

162
00:14:05.450 --> 00:14:11.980
The bodies were loaded onto barges, carried out past the mouth of the river, weighted down and released into the sea.

163
00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:16.750
For a devout society this was a grave decision, but it held off an epidemic.

164
00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:19.920
The second danger was hunger and disorder.

165
00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:24.850
Pombal brought army units into the city and sealed off its exits.

166
00:14:24.850 --> 00:14:30.100
The aim was to stop able-bodied men from leaving and to put them to work clearing the rubble.

167
00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:32.630
He dealt very harshly with looting.

168
00:14:32.630 --> 00:14:40.120
Gallows were set up at various points around the city, and those caught looting were executed in public as a warning to others.

169
00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:43.290
This severity was debated even at the time.

170
00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:46.460
But order was soon restored.

171
00:14:46.460 --> 00:14:52.190
At the same time, ceilings were placed on food prices, and profiteering was banned.

172
00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:59.200
Grain was brought in from the surrounding regions, and provisions aboard ships arriving in the harbor were seized and distributed.

173
00:14:59.200 --> 00:15:03.010
Taxes on fishermen were temporarily lifted.

174
00:15:03.010 --> 00:15:05.700
Shelters were built for the homeless.

175
00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:08.550
Help also came from across Europe.

176
00:15:08.550 --> 00:15:14.600
When the news reached London, the British Parliament set aside one hundred thousand pounds in aid for Portugal.

177
00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:19.050
Ships carried grain, flour, rice and tools.

178
00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:21.260
Spain sent help as well.

179
00:15:21.260 --> 00:15:26.990
Faced with the destruction of a capital, allies and rivals stood on the same side for a brief moment.

180
00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:30.960
Pombal's most ambitious step, though, was the rebuilding.

181
00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:36.210
Many proposed moving the capital somewhere else, to Belém, for example.

182
00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:38.260
Pombal opposed this.

183
00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:43.190
Lisbon would be rebuilt on the same site, but as an entirely different city.

184
00:15:43.190 --> 00:15:48.280
For the task, he appointed the kingdom's chief engineer, Manuel da Maia.

185
00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:54.170
Maia, then nearly eighty years old, prepared several different options for the reconstruction.

186
00:15:54.170 --> 00:16:03.580
The plan chosen was the most radical one: the ruined center would be cleared completely, the old street layout erased, and a brand-new city drawn in its place.

187
00:16:03.580 --> 00:16:09.630
The architects Eugénio dos Santos and Carlos Mardel worked out the details of the plan.

188
00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:14.880
What emerged was an urban planning project with few parallels anywhere in Europe.

189
00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:23.490
The lower town, stretching from the riverfront to Rossio Square, was rebuilt on a grid of straight, broad streets crossing one another at right angles.

190
00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:27.140
Today the district still bears Pombal's name.

191
00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:33.990
Building heights were limited, and the facades were designed to the same dimensions, in a plain and sober style.

192
00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:41.000
Door and window frames, iron railings, even the roof beams were made in standard sizes and prepared in advance.

193
00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:46.570
It was a kind of early mass production, and it was this that set the pace of construction.

194
00:16:46.570 --> 00:16:50.380
Inside the new buildings, an innovation lay hidden.

195
00:16:50.380 --> 00:16:56.750
Within the walls, builders set a skeleton of timber beams braced diagonally against one another.

196
00:16:56.750 --> 00:16:59.920
This system is known as the Pombaline cage.

197
00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:02.930
The idea was simple, but brilliant.

198
00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:10.420
Even if the stone walls cracked during an earthquake, the flexible timber frame would keep the building standing and give people time to escape.

199
00:17:10.420 --> 00:17:20.790
According to the accounts, the engineers had scale models of the system built, then marched companies of soldiers in step around them to create an artificial tremor and watched how the models behaved.

200
00:17:20.790 --> 00:17:26.840
This building technique is considered one of the first systematic examples of earthquake-resistant design.

201
00:17:26.840 --> 00:17:32.090
Tall firewalls were also added between buildings to stop fire from spreading.

202
00:17:32.090 --> 00:17:36.220
Where the old Palace Square had stood, a new square rose.

203
00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:38.270
The palace was gone.

204
00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:42.880
In its place stood government offices, the customs house and the exchange.

205
00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:45.730
The square was renamed Commerce Square.

206
00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:51.780
Even the new name carried a message: the heart of Lisbon was no longer the royal palace, but trade.

207
00:17:51.780 --> 00:17:56.870
At the centre of the square stood an equestrian statue of King Joseph the First.

208
00:17:56.870 --> 00:18:05.320
The great triumphal arch linking the square to the main street of the lower town, however, was not completed until much later, in eighteen seventy-five.

209
00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:10.890
During all this, Pombal took one more step, and it may have been his most far-sighted.

210
00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:16.780
He sent a detailed list of questions to every parish in the country, that is, to the local priests.

211
00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:22.030
The questions went like this: How many minutes did the earthquake last?

212
00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:24.560
How many aftershocks were felt?

213
00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:27.570
From which direction did it seem to come?

214
00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:29.940
Which buildings collapsed?

215
00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:32.470
How many people died?

216
00:18:32.470 --> 00:18:36.760
What changes were seen at sea, and how high did the water rise?

217
00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:40.250
Did the water level in the wells change?

218
00:18:40.250 --> 00:18:43.900
Did animals behave strangely before the earthquake?

219
00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:47.070
Did cracks open in the ground?

220
00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:51.520
The replies are preserved today in the National Archives of Portugal.

221
00:18:51.520 --> 00:19:03.170
Because this was one of the first attempts to document an earthquake across such a wide area with systematic questions, some historians of science now regard Pombal as one of the pioneers of modern seismology.

222
00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:06.820
But rebuilding was not only a matter of stone and timber.

223
00:19:06.820 --> 00:19:10.950
A struggle had also begun over what the disaster meant.

224
00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:15.720
In Lisbon, many clergymen preached that the earthquake was a punishment from God.

225
00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:20.810
The most influential of them was the Italian-born Jesuit priest Gabriel Malagrida.

226
00:19:20.810 --> 00:19:30.380
In a tract published in seventeen fifty-six, Malagrida wrote that the true cause of the earthquake was the city's sins, and that those seeking natural explanations were mistaken.

227
00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:34.990
He urged the people to turn to penance and retreat rather than work on rebuilding.

228
00:19:34.990 --> 00:19:38.800
This message clashed with Pombal's entire program.

229
00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:45.650
Pombal was seeking a natural explanation for the earthquake, and he opposed anything that might slow the pace of reconstruction.

230
00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:50.900
Malagrida was first removed from the court, then banished from the capital.

231
00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:58.390
Years later he was linked to an attempt on the king's life, tried by the Inquisition, and executed in seventeen sixty-one.

232
00:19:58.390 --> 00:20:03.160
This clash was part of Pombal's wider quarrel with the Jesuits.

233
00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:09.210
In seventeen fifty-nine, the Jesuit order was expelled from Portugal altogether.

234
00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:16.380
Yet the real debate over the meaning of Lisbon took place far beyond Portugal's borders, in the intellectual world of Europe.

235
00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:22.590
In the middle of the eighteenth century, a powerful optimism held sway in Europe's educated circles.

236
00:20:22.590 --> 00:20:29.120
The German philosopher Leibniz had argued that the world God created was the best of all possible worlds.

237
00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:35.330
By this way of thinking, the evils in the world were part of a greater good that we simply cannot see.

238
00:20:35.330 --> 00:20:42.660
The English poet Alexander Pope had put a similar idea into verse: the notion that whatever is, is right.

239
00:20:42.660 --> 00:20:49.510
In philosophy, this debate is called theodicy, the effort to defend God's goodness in the face of the evil in the world.

240
00:20:49.510 --> 00:20:52.840
Lisbon shook this optimism to its core.

241
00:20:52.840 --> 00:21:02.250
Churches collapsing on the faithful at Mass on the morning of a holy day, some of the districts that housed the city's brothels left standing, innocent children dead.

242
00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:05.900
What greater good could all of this possibly be part of?

243
00:21:05.900 --> 00:21:11.950
The French writer and thinker Voltaire was among those who asked this question most loudly.

244
00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:20.400
A few weeks after hearing the news, he wrote a long poem called Poem on the Lisbon Disaster, and published it in seventeen fifty-six.

245
00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:25.810
In it, he told the philosophers who claimed that all is well to come and look at the ruins of Lisbon.

246
00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:32.340
He asked what crime the dead had committed, and why Lisbon should be thought more sinful than London or Paris.

247
00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:35.510
Voltaire did not reject belief in God.

248
00:21:35.510 --> 00:21:41.080
What he rejected was the comfort of brushing suffering aside with a ready-made explanation.

249
00:21:41.080 --> 00:21:45.210
Jean-Jacques Rousseau gave the most striking reply to this poem.

250
00:21:45.210 --> 00:21:49.980
In the summer of seventeen fifty-six, he wrote Voltaire a long letter.

251
00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:55.390
In Rousseau's view, people bore part of the blame for how great the disaster had been.

252
00:21:55.390 --> 00:22:03.680
Nature had not piled thousands of houses, six and seven storeys high, on top of one another in that narrow space; people had done that.

253
00:22:03.680 --> 00:22:09.250
Had they lived in houses that were more spread out and lower, far fewer lives would have been lost.

254
00:22:09.250 --> 00:22:16.900
What's more, many had gone back into collapsing houses to save their belongings, their papers, their money, and had died there.

255
00:22:16.900 --> 00:22:22.630
Rousseau was moving the debate away from the justice of God and toward the choices people make for themselves.

256
00:22:22.630 --> 00:22:30.440
Today, disaster science holds that a catastrophe comes not only from a natural event, but also from how and where people live.

257
00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:35.210
Rousseau's letter is considered one of the earliest statements of this idea.

258
00:22:35.210 --> 00:22:41.740
Voltaire gave his answer years later, in seventeen fifty-nine, in the form of a novel: Candide.

259
00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:46.990
Its young hero, Candide, travels the world with his tutor, Pangloss.

260
00:22:46.990 --> 00:22:54.160
Whatever befalls them, Pangloss keeps repeating that everything happens for the best in the best of all possible worlds.

261
00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:57.010
Their travels take them to Lisbon as well.

262
00:22:57.010 --> 00:23:00.980
They arrive in the city just as the earthquake strikes.

263
00:23:00.980 --> 00:23:07.350
Afterward, the authorities decide to burn a few people in a solemn ceremony to prevent further earthquakes.

264
00:23:07.350 --> 00:23:11.000
Pangloss is hanged, and Candide is flogged.

265
00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:14.170
Voltaire wrote this scene with dark humor.

266
00:23:14.170 --> 00:23:21.020
He was mocking two things at once: philosophical optimism, and the belief that disaster is divine punishment.

267
00:23:21.020 --> 00:23:31.710
Candide soon became one of the most widely read books in Europe, and the advice at the novel's end, that one must cultivate one's own garden, came to be read as a modest answer to catastrophe.

268
00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:38.720
Another figure who joined the debate was Immanuel Kant, then in his early thirties and still an unknown teacher in Königsberg.

269
00:23:38.720 --> 00:23:44.450
In seventeen fifty-six, Kant published three short essays on earthquakes.

270
00:23:44.450 --> 00:23:51.460
In them, he looked for the cause of earthquakes not in divine punishment, but in natural processes beneath the ground.

271
00:23:51.460 --> 00:23:58.470
He proposed that vast underground caverns filled with hot gases existed below the surface, and that earthquakes arose from them.

272
00:23:58.470 --> 00:24:06.280
That explanation is now known to be wrong, but his approach mattered: an earthquake was a natural event that needed to be explained.

273
00:24:06.280 --> 00:24:17.450
A few years later, the English scientist John Michell proposed that earthquakes were waves travelling through the Earth's crust, and that their point of origin could be found by looking at the times the waves arrived.

274
00:24:17.450 --> 00:24:26.700
Pombal's questionnaire, Kant's essays and Michell's idea of waves were different steps, taken after the same disaster, all pointing in the same direction.

275
00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:30.670
The Lisbon earthquake also left its mark on a child's memory.

276
00:24:30.670 --> 00:24:36.240
Johann Wolfgang von Goethe was six years old at the time and living in Frankfurt.

277
00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:43.730
Years later, in his memoir Poetry and Truth, he described how this news shattered the peace of his young mind for the first time.

278
00:24:43.730 --> 00:24:53.460
He remembered being unable to reconcile what he heard in the grown-ups' conversations, the idea of a wise and merciful creator, with the deaths of so many innocent people.

279
00:24:53.460 --> 00:24:58.390
This is why some historians of ideas call Lisbon the first modern disaster.

280
00:24:58.390 --> 00:25:01.080
There are several reasons for this.

281
00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:10.490
First, thanks to printing presses, newspapers and networks of letters, the news had spread across all of Europe, creating a shared shock throughout the continent.

282
00:25:10.490 --> 00:25:20.700
Second, in the wake of the disaster, a state had responded not with prayer and penance, but with a planned program of relief, record-keeping and reconstruction.

283
00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:28.670
Third, and perhaps most important, the world of philosophy had begun to draw an ever sharper line between natural evil and moral evil.

284
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:36.960
An earthquake was no longer seen as punishment for someone's sins, but as an event to be understood, measured and, where possible, guarded against.

285
00:25:36.960 --> 00:25:40.930
Pombal's own fortunes, however, rose and fell.

286
00:25:40.930 --> 00:25:45.700
Over the next twenty years, he ruled Portugal almost single-handedly.

287
00:25:45.700 --> 00:25:50.950
The order he built is remembered both for its reforming side and for its very harsh one.

288
00:25:50.950 --> 00:26:00.680
In seventeen seventy-seven, when Joseph the First died and his daughter, Maria the First, took the throne, Pombal fell from favor and spent his remaining years in exile.

289
00:26:00.680 --> 00:26:03.530
But the city he built still stands.

290
00:26:03.530 --> 00:26:10.380
Today, anyone walking through Lisbon's lower town is walking through the decisions made two hundred and seventy years ago.

291
00:26:10.380 --> 00:26:18.990
The straight streets, the plain, matching facades and the broad Commerce Square opening onto the river are the fabric of the city built after the disaster.

292
00:26:18.990 --> 00:26:25.200
Inside the walls of some of these buildings, the wooden frame of the Pombaline cage still stands.

293
00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:30.770
Engineers study this system as an important link in the history of earthquake-resistant construction.

294
00:26:30.770 --> 00:26:37.780
At the same time, there are warnings that some of these frames were weakened by renovations carried out in later periods.

295
00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:43.830
In other words, the lesson of seventeen fifty-five is still a live debate in Lisbon today.

296
00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:49.240
The roofless church of the Carmo Convent on the hill, meanwhile, was deliberately never rebuilt.

297
00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:56.570
Its Gothic arches stand open to the sky, just as the earthquake left them, and today it houses an archaeology museum.

298
00:26:56.570 --> 00:27:02.460
Where the roof once fell on those who had come to Mass that feast-day morning, there is now only sky.

299
00:27:02.460 --> 00:27:05.150
The ocean has not forgotten either.

300
00:27:05.150 --> 00:27:12.000
The fault zone off Portugal's southwest coast is still active today, and scientists keep it under constant watch.

301
00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:21.090
The tsunami warning systems built for the northeastern Atlantic and the Mediterranean were shaped, in part, by the memory of seventeen fifty-five.

302
00:27:21.090 --> 00:27:27.300
In Lisbon's waterfront neighborhoods today, there are signs marking the tsunami evacuation routes.

303
00:27:27.300 --> 00:27:34.950
In school drills, students are taught that when the sea pulls back, they should run not toward the shore, but toward higher ground.

304
00:27:34.950 --> 00:27:43.080
What the crowd that took refuge in the Palace Square in seventeen fifty-five did not know is now the first rule taught to every child.

305
00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:46.250
The Lisbon earthquake destroyed a city.

306
00:27:46.250 --> 00:27:51.980
It wiped out a library, a palace, an opera house, and tens of thousands of lives.

307
00:27:51.980 --> 00:27:56.270
But from among the ruins, three new questions emerged.

308
00:27:56.270 --> 00:28:00.240
The engineers asked: how can this be prevented?

309
00:28:00.240 --> 00:28:04.210
The scientists asked: why does this happen?

310
00:28:04.210 --> 00:28:07.860
The philosophers asked: does this have any meaning?

311
00:28:07.860 --> 00:28:12.790
Since that day, the first two questions have found better and better answers.

312
00:28:12.790 --> 00:28:20.280
The third, picking up where Voltaire, Rousseau and Kant left off, is still asked anew after every great catastrophe.

313
00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:24.250
We have come to the end of today's episode of Nöron Tarih.

314
00:28:24.250 --> 00:28:27.580
Until we meet again in a new story.
